WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:05.360
Hey, Wisecrack.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.390
Just your friendly neighborhood Jared here.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:13.219
Confession: I’ve watched every single episode of
the 1977 Spider-Man TV show…

00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:14.220
MORE THAN ONCE.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:15.929
Don’t judge me.

00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:18.160
…Ok, maybe your judgment is earned…

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:19.600
"No it isnt. This shit is awesome."

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:22.160
But in all seriousness – I really dig Spiderman.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:26.640
And the thing that constantly draws me back,
no matter how many times the franchise is

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.880
rebooted, is just how AMERICAN Spider-Man
truly is.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.080
"Last time you did the laundry you turned everything blue and red!"

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:37.100
"That was a mistake cause... I was washing the American Flag"

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:41.140
Now I don’t mean that Spidey loves pickup
trucks and double cheeseburgers, but rather,

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:44.360
that he embodies fundamental elements of American
ideology.

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.100
"Captain... big fan, I'm spider man."

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:52.200
So cue that patriotic music and welcome to
this ‘amazingly’ Ameri-CAN Wisecrack Edition

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.300
on The Philosophy of Spider-Man.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.449
Now, yes, many superheroes from Batman to
Captain America, embody different aspects

00:00:59.449 --> 00:01:03.449
of American values: standing up for the little
guy, fighting for what’s right, adhering

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:07.750
to a strict moral code; but Spider-Man does
so in a way that uniquely reflects America’s

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:09.380
role in the world.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:14.100
Just look at Peter Parker - a young kid, coming
of age, who’s suddenly gifted an insane

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.250
amount of power.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:19.970
He’s constantly wrestling with how to wield
this power or, well, if he even should.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:25.680
Parker mirrors America-- a young country,
that post-WWII happened upon a massive amount

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.250
of power, and constantly grapples with how
to responsibly wield this newfound power - if

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.560
it should at all.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.329
America doesn’t have a long history to draw
upon for moral direction just as Peter doesn’t

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:38.020
have any parents to guide him.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:41.890
In fact -- the only piece of advice Peter’s
ever given comes from Uncle Ben.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:42.680
You know the line…

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.579
Uncle Ben’s famous words reflect many ideas
inherent to American Exceptionalism, you know,

00:01:51.579 --> 00:01:56.229
like the idea that America is dope af and
thus has a duty to lead the world.

00:01:56.229 --> 00:02:01.350
This idea is encapsulated by “The city upon
a hill,” a metaphor taken from the New Testament

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:06.520
by American colonists to describe their mission
- to be a beacon of hope to the rest of the world.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.600
It's use still continues, for example:

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:14.860
"The eyes of all people are truly upon us and our governments, must be as a city upon a hill,

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:20.360
constructed and inhabited by men aware of their great trust and their great responsibilities."

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.020
...Or This Ronald Reagan Speech.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:28.200
"The past few days when I've been at that window upstairs, I've thought a bit about that shining city upon a hill."

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.780
This view of America has been reflected in
each Spider-Man film -- from Tobey Maguire

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.870
to Andrew Garfield to likely Tom Holland.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.810
In each adaptation, Peter struggles with the
weight of his sudden powers yet feels a moral

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:46.310
responsibility to use them justly, a dilemma
that mirrors two American philosophies: The

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.030
Reluctant Sheriff and The American Jeremiad.

00:02:48.030 --> 00:02:52.870
‘The Reluctant Sheriff’, first proposed
by American Diplomat Richard Haass, basically

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:58.010
takes your standard grizzled Clint Eastwood
cowboy and applies it to U.S global policy.

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:02.280
The U.S. doesn’t want to enforce international
order, but since there isn’t anybody else

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:06.700
as powerful, we gotta suck it up and govern
the shit out of the rest of the world.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.840
Put simply, the U.S doesn’t want to regulate
global politics, but must do so to prevent

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.420
the rising tide of violence.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.800
Similarly, Peter Parker, both the McGuire
and Garfield versions, isn’t even sure he

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:21.560
wants to be ‘Spider-Man’, yet ultimately
feels obligated.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:25.610
Much of the drama stems from Peter’s struggle
to balance his personal life with his duty

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:26.790
as a superhero.

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.510
In Spider-Man, Peter barely makes it to his
Thanksgiving dinner because he’s recovering

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.450
from a fight with the Green Goblin.

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:36.400
And in The Amazing Spider-Man 2, Peter’s late
to his own high-school graduation because

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.620
he’s gotta fight Paul Giamatti or something.

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:43.200
According to Haass, The ‘Reluctant Sheriff’ must
also create a “posse”, people who work

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.600
with the sheriff for the good of their ‘town.’

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.300
Spiderman works with New Yorkers to create his own kinda posse.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:53.640
In Spider-Man, New Yorkers throw debris off
the Queensboro Bridge to distract The Green

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:58.360
Goblin in Spider-Man 2, the train commuters
keep Spider-Man’s identity a secret;

00:03:58.360 --> 00:03:59.820
"We won't tell nobody."

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:05.720
in The Amazing Spider-Man, all the NYC Crane Operators
aid Spidey in web-slinging across town; and

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:10.650
in The Amazing Spider-Man 2, the fire department
helps Spider-Man to fight Electro.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:14.190
Even still, the weight of being Spider-Man
is too much for Peter.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:19.100
In Spider-Man 2, Peter just flat out says
he can no longer carry on being a superhero

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:20.840
because it’s ruining his personal life.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.560
And in The Amazing Spider-Man 2, Peter makes
a similar confession to Gwen,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.580
and here’s where The American Jeremiad kicks in.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:45.100
According to thinkers Sacvan Bercovitch and
William V. Spanos, The American Jeremiad emphasizes

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.870
that it’s the U.S.’s God-given mission
to save this world.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:52.070
It’s like the reluctant sheriff, minus the
reluctance.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:56.260
If we lose our focus for even half-a-second,
or think that someone else might pick up the

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:01.070
slack of of spreading democracy, godliness
&amp; morality -- the world could literally end...

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:05.630
Which, to a lesser extent, is exactly what
happens in each Spider-Man film.

00:05:05.630 --> 00:05:06.940
Take Uncle Ben’s death.

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.580
When Peter lets a thief get away,

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.340
"You coulda taken that guy apart!"

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:12.140
"I missed the part where that's my problem."

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:15.420
his moral inaction leads to his uncle’s demise, forever

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:20.000
reminding Peter that if he doesn’t live
up to his ethical mission, people will die.

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:25.600
Likewise when Peter quits being ‘Spider-Man’,
the bad-guy power vacuum immediately fills in.

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:30.400
In Spider-Man 2, during our hero’s absence,
the crime rate spikes and Doc Ock is able

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.240
to rebuild his doomsday reactor.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.060
When Doc captures Mary Jane and the reactor
becomes an existential threat to the city,

00:05:37.060 --> 00:05:41.020
Peter realizes he MUST be Spiderman to save
millions of lives.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:45.700
Even after Peter and MJ become official, they
recognize that it’s his DUTY to protect

00:05:45.700 --> 00:05:46.320
the city.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:47.680
"Go get em, tiger."

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:52.420
Similarly, After Gwen’s death forces Peter
to quit in The Amazing Spider-Man 2, the Green

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:58.000
Goblin builds up a network of super-villains;
including the weird mech-rhino Giamatti dude.

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.060
The film ends with Peter realizing that without
Spiderman, the city is doomed to escalating

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.120
violence.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:08.900
Even with this consistency between adaptations,
some elements seem to shift in conjunction

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.420
with American values.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:14.370
After all, the political climates surrounding
each series were pretty different, so it’s

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:18.250
not surprising that there would be a shift
in how heroics are displayed.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:22.360
Tobey Maguire’s Spider-Man largely uses
what’s known in international relations

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:27.430
as hard power— he rushes into battle, relying
on brute strength to stop his foes.

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:31.950
There’s no time for negotiation with The
Green Goblin, Doc Ock and The Sandman.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:34.290
Just some good ol’ fashioned fisticuffs.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:38.840
Andrew Garfield, however, offers a “soft
power” version of Spider-Man—he’s constantly

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:41.860
trying to persuade his enemies to stop their
nonsense.

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:43.880
"I can see that you don't want to hurt anybody."

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:48.980
This difference highlights a central contradiction
within the discourse of American Identity—moral

00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:52.900
righteousness through strength versus moral
righteousness through leadership.

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:56.780
In the face of 9/11, moral absolutes were
demanded with the Bush Administration’s

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:59.100
“with us or against us” rhetoric.

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:03.550
Obama’s election, however, brought with
it a youthful feel, tied to messages of “hope”

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:04.550
and “change”.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:09.200
As such, Garfield’s Spider-Man uses more
persuasive tactics to deal with foes.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:16.020
"On behalf of the fine people of New York City and real rhinos everywhere, I ask you to put your mechanized paws in the air."

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.700
So how will the newest iteration reflect the
American values of today?

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:24.620
Well -- there’s really no telling until
we see Homecoming, but it seems that Tom Holland’s

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:29.540
Spiderman may distance itself from the American
Jeremiad or Reluctant Sheriff.

00:07:29.540 --> 00:07:34.520
Unlike the Maguire &amp; Garfield films, Holland’s
Spider-Man is no longer the only hero on

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.280
the block, but one of many.

00:07:36.280 --> 00:07:41.240
This shift reflects different models of how
the global stage can, and should operate.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:45.930
You see, in a “unipolar world,” there
is one central power that keeps everybody

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:47.010
in line.

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:51.680
Many argue that our current world is exactly
this, and the US is not only that central

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.419
power, but it SHOULD be the that central power
aka- The American Jeremiad.

00:07:56.419 --> 00:08:00.240
But the world wasn’t always unipolar, and
some argue it shouldn’t be.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.270
In a multi-polar world, there is no head honcho,
but an assortment of nations with varying

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:09.240
economic and military strength who who coalesce
into alliances.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.320
Sort of like, say, the Avengers.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:16.670
Holland’s Spider-Man, now just one of many
super-heroes, imagines this multi-polar vision.

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:21.260
He’s no longer needed to be the beacon of
hope and goodness because there’s a dozen

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.310
other heroes who can pick up the slack.

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:27.520
Heck, Spider-Man’s even told by Stark to
“sit out” the remainder of the Civil War fight.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:29.760
"Go on home, I'll call Aunt May. You're done."

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:30.700
"Hey, I'm not."

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:34.820
In the trailers for Spider-Man: Homecoming
– Tony double-downs on this advice, telling

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:38.900
Peter that he’s in way over his head and
that he should just stay in New York.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:46.670
Aside from the political shift between Maguire-man
and Garfield-man, there’s also an economic

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:49.070
shift reflected between the two series.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:53.130
The Maguire films constantly highlight Peter’s
working-class status.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:57.320
Peter lives in a small house, within a blue
collar neighborhood, that Aunt May struggles

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:58.320
to pay off.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.460
His poor Uncle Ben has recently been fired

00:09:00.460 --> 00:09:04.860
"Corporation is downsizing the people and upsizing their profits."

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.200
and everyone in the family is looking for work.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:12.880
In Spider-Man 2, Peter fails as a pizza delivery
boy, lives in a flophouse with a shared bathroom,

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:15.160
and makes a pittance from the Daily Bugle.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:20.280
Yet the original trilogy valorizes Peter’s
poverty, endowing objects like Aunt May’s

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:25.420
modest wedding ring with a certain sentimental
value that trumps any kind of monetary value.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:28.220
"Hello."

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:31.160
Peter’s money problems are often used as
a source of levity

00:09:31.160 --> 00:09:35.780
"I'm really sorry Mr. Ditkovich, you know, all I got is this twenty for the rest of the week."

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:37.460
"Sorry doesn't pay the rent."

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.160
playfully affirming the idea that struggling
is just a step towards The American Dream.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.920
Just look at the difference between Peter
and Eddie Brock.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:49.980
Peter keeps his head down and just does the
work; whereas antagonist Eddie uses flattery

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:54.780
"I had this stupid little dream of working with one of the greatest newspaper editors of our time."

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:56.800
and fake photos to get ahead.

00:09:56.810 --> 00:10:01.640
Of course - by the end of Spider-Man 3, Eddie
gets unceremoniously fired and then, well,

00:10:01.640 --> 00:10:02.870
blown the hell up.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:07.529
The valorization of humble poverty becomes
especially obvious at the end of Spider-Man

00:10:07.529 --> 00:10:13.310
2, when Mary-Jane rushes away from her marriage
to a successful astronaut and chooses instead

00:10:13.310 --> 00:10:15.940
to be with the broke-as-a-joke Peter.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:21.040
This resonates with the early 2000’s economy—
while it wasn't terrific, there was arguably

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.390
a semblance of hope.

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:26.800
The idea of keeping your head down and working
your way out of poverty was a bit more popular

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.960
than it would be during and after the Great
Recession.

00:10:29.960 --> 00:10:34.810
Yet by the time The Amazing Spider-Man came
around in 2012 - four years after the 2008

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:38.250
Great Recession, being “poor” lost much
of its glamor.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:43.120
The housing crisis that Aunt May fictionally
fights against in Spider-Man 2 becomes all

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:45.050
too real for much of the US.

00:10:45.050 --> 00:10:48.940
As such, Peter’s ‘poverty’ is rarely
spoken of in these films.

00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:54.240
His dad’s now a rich scientist with a private
jet; and his Uncle Ben and Aunt May have a

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:55.360
pretty nice house.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.720
Instead The Amazing Spider-Man highlights
Peter’s nerdy ingenuity.

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.680
He’s a science wizard, designing his own
web-shooters and assisting the genius Dr.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:04.710
Connors at Oscorp.

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:08.310
This Peter doesn’t have to grind away at
Joe’s Pizza.

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.190
So what will Spiderman Homecoming have in
store for us?

00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:15.649
We’ve seen major political changes in the
last year, and the economy seems to be doing

00:11:15.649 --> 00:11:18.130
pretty well or at least, the stock market
is.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:23.251
Will Homecoming reflect this in its own unique
way, or just offer us some good ol’ fashion

00:11:23.251 --> 00:11:24.830
mindless asskicking?

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:28.690
Will the younger Aunt May be characterized
as a struggling single parent?

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:30.700
Will the Daily Bugle look like Buzzfeed?

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:32.000
Let us know what you think in the comments.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:33.600
Thanks for watching guys. Peace!

